8,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,49454,323,14927,1817,1744,315,264,79151,47311,13,2014,862,12761,11,2176,29136,28370,1191,400,87,61,17,10,19,87,41715,4385,2412,12947,576,11341,315,279,49319,517,11,400,65,61,17,12,19,580,54876,315,16247,594,47311,311,279,49319,517,315,14927,594,47311,374,6144,311,279,11341,315,14927,594,6783,4647,311,16247,594,6783,4647,13,1416,862,6783,3793,525,537,6144,11,1477,279,2629,315,279,6783,3793,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nEric and Charles each think of a quadratic polynomial. To their surprise, both quadratics start $x^2+4x+\\cdots$. The ratio of the discriminant, $b^2-4ac$, of Eric's polynomial to the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. If their constant terms are not equal, find the sum of the constant terms.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"54ed9d3dc559964fe71eb98c1e0fc2f66cd8d899a9b66bfe4ebf42deac99ab59"},"seconds":36.03295969963074,"seed":259257197394455363,"stop":"eos","text":"Let's denote Eric's polynomial as \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\) and Charles's polynomial as \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the constant terms of Eric's and Charles's polynomials, respectively.\n\nThe general form of a quadratic polynomial is \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\). The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic polynomial \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\).\n\nFor Eric's polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + a\\), the discriminant \\(\\Delta_E\\) is:\n\\[\n\\Delta_E = 4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot a = 16 - 4a\n\\]\n\nFor Charles's polynomial \\(x^2 + 4x + b\\), the discriminant \\(\\Delta_C\\) is:\n\\[\n\\Delta_C = 4^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot b = 16 - 4b\n\\]\n\nAccording to the problem, the ratio of the discriminant of Eric's polynomial to the discriminant of Charles's polynomial is equal to the ratio of Charles's constant term to Eric's constant term. This can be written as:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta_E}{\\Delta_C} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the discriminants into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{16 - 4a}{16 - 4b} = \\frac{4(4 - a)}{4(4 - b)} = \\frac{4 - a}{4 - b}\n\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{4 - a}{4 - b} = \\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying to clear the fractions:\n\\[\na(4 - a) = b(4 - b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\\[\n4a - a^2 = 4b - b^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging the terms:\n\\[\na^2 - b^2 = 4a - 4b\n\\]\n\nFactoring both sides:\n\\[\n(a - b)(a + b) = 4(a - b)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a \\neq b\\), we can divide both sides by \\(a - b\\):\n\\[\na + b = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the constant terms \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
